const TUTOR_LESSONS = {"reviewed":"2026-10-22","scope":"Site-authored study notes, not individual operational advice or exhaustive course coverage.","lessons":[{"id":"pilot-decision","title":"Deciding whether to pause an AI pilot","aliases":["pilot stop criteria","pilot go/no-go"],"questions":["What evidence should an AI product manager use to stop a pilot?","When should we stop an AI pilot?"],"career":"ai-product-manager","section":"roadmap","plain":"Compare the pilot results with the limits agreed before the test: answer quality, harmful mistakes, response time, cost and user benefit. A failed required check is a reason to pause or stop and investigate. Passing a check is not a reason to stop. Passing every check supports a review of whether to continue; it does not prove the product is safe in every situation. Record the results, affected users, remaining risks and who owns the decision. This lesson cannot decide your actual pilot without its agreed limits and evidence.","professional":"Use pre-agreed go/no-go criteria and representative evaluation evidence: quality, safety, latency, unit cost and user outcomes. A breached mandatory criterion triggers the agreed pause/stop and escalation process; meeting a criterion is not stop evidence. Passing gates supports a scoped continuation review, not a universal safety claim. Document uncertainty, affected segments, residual risk and the accountable decision owner. The actual decision requires the pilot’s thresholds and evidence.","hint":"List the mandatory checks first. For each one, mark pass, fail or unknown. Which failed or unresolved check requires a pause under your agreed decision process?","quiz":{"question":"A pilot meets its response-time target but fails a mandatory harmful-output check. Should the fast responses justify continuing unchanged?","answer":"No. Meeting the response-time target does not cancel the failed mandatory safety check. Follow the agreed pause or stop process, investigate the failures and gather evidence before an accountable owner reviews a restart. The exact thresholds belong to the pilot plan."},"source":{"name":"NIST AI RMF 1.0 · Manage 1–2 (page 32)","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf#page=37"},"phase":2},{"id":"citations","title":"Why a citation is not proof","aliases":["citation correctness","groundedness versus correctness"],"questions":["Does a citation guarantee an answer is correct?","Can a cited answer be wrong?"],"career":"evals-engineer","section":"roadmap","plain":"A source link tells you where to check a statement. It does not prove that the statement follows from the source. An answer may cite the right page but reverse its meaning, leave out a condition or use outdated information. Read the relevant passage and check that it supports the exact claim. When the passage does not answer the question, say there is not enough evidence.","professional":"Evaluate citation validity, claim-to-evidence support and factual correctness separately. A valid URL or source ID does not establish entailment: a response can reverse a condition, omit scope or cite stale material. Check the claim against the passage and its applicability. Abstain when the evidence does not support the requested conclusion.","hint":"Compare the verbs and conditions in the claim with the source. Does “fails” accidentally become “passes”? Does a limited finding become an unconditional promise?","quiz":{"question":"An answer links to the right document but reverses “deny access” into “allow access”. Is the answer supported?","answer":"No. The link is valid, but the claim contradicts the passage. Reject or correct the claim; checking only the source ID would miss this error."},"source":{"name":"Microsoft: RAG design and evaluation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-solution-design-and-evaluation-guide"},"phase":0},{"id":"rag-training","title":"Retrieval and fine-tuning do different jobs","aliases":["RAG versus fine-tuning","retrieval versus training"],"questions":["Does RAG train the model?","Does adding a document to RAG change model weights?"],"career":"retrieval-engineer","section":"roadmap","plain":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) finds relevant passages and includes them in the model’s input for an answer. Adding a document to a search index does not by itself train or change the model’s learned weights. Fine-tuning changes learned weights using training examples. A system can use both. Retrieved text can still be irrelevant or wrong, so the answer needs evaluation.","professional":"RAG supplies retrieved context at inference time; indexing a document does not by itself update model parameters. Fine-tuning optimizes parameters on training examples. The approaches can be combined, but retrieval quality and answer support still require evaluation.","hint":"Ask which thing changes: the information supplied with a request, or the model’s learned parameters?","quiz":{"question":"You add a PDF to a retrieval index without running training. Have you fine-tuned the language model?","answer":"No. You changed the searchable context. Fine-tuning requires a training process that updates model parameters. A later request may retrieve the PDF, but that is not the same as learning it into the weights."},"source":{"name":"Microsoft: RAG design and evaluation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-solution-design-and-evaluation-guide"},"phase":2},{"id":"readiness","title":"Readiness is not answer correctness","aliases":["readiness versus correctness","Kubernetes readiness"],"questions":["Does a passing readiness probe prove a model is accurate?"],"career":"platform","section":"roadmap","plain":"A Kubernetes readiness check helps decide whether a container is ready to receive traffic. When a Pod is not ready, Kubernetes removes it from the matching Service’s endpoints. Passing a health check does not prove that model answers are accurate or safe. You need separate tests of model behavior. A failed readiness check alone does not restart the container; liveness and startup checks have different roles.","professional":"Readiness controls whether a Pod is eligible for Service traffic; an unready Pod is removed from matching Service endpoints. A successful probe is not a model-quality or safety evaluation. Readiness failure alone does not trigger a container restart; liveness and startup probe failures have distinct restart behavior.","hint":"Separate two questions: can this instance accept traffic, and does its model produce acceptable results?","quiz":{"question":"A /health endpoint returns 200 while the model gives incorrect answers. Does that contradict a successful readiness check?","answer":"No. The configured health check may only test serving readiness. Model correctness needs separate behavioral evaluation; improve the checks according to their intended purpose."},"source":{"name":"Kubernetes: container probes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/probes/"},"phase":2},{"id":"drift","title":"Drift is a signal to investigate","aliases":["drift versus accuracy","data drift"],"questions":["Does data drift prove model accuracy fell?","Should I always retrain when drift is detected?"],"career":"mlops","section":"roadmap","plain":"Data drift means incoming data has changed compared with a reference. That change can affect model quality, but it does not by itself prove accuracy has fallen. Check data quality, affected groups and task results using suitable labels or other evidence. Decide whether to fix the data, adjust the system or retrain based on the cause. Test a new model before replacing the current one.","professional":"A distribution-shift signal is not direct evidence of degraded predictive performance. Validate the input pipeline, affected slices and labeled performance where available; account for label delays. Choose remediation from the root cause and evaluate a retrained candidate before promotion.","hint":"What changed in the input, and what evidence shows that the model’s useful results changed too?","quiz":{"question":"A drift alert fires, but no new outcome labels are available. Can you confidently claim that accuracy fell by 20%?","answer":"No. The alert shows a measured distribution change, not a measured accuracy drop. Investigate the data and collect suitable performance evidence; disclose the uncertainty."},"source":{"name":"Google Cloud: MLOps pipelines","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning"},"phase":3},{"id":"evaluation-split","title":"Keep the final test separate","aliases":["test set leakage","training validation test split"],"questions":["Can I tune a model on the final test set?"],"career":"ml-engineer","section":"roadmap","plain":"Training data is used to fit the model. Validation data helps compare choices while you develop it. Keep a final test set separate to estimate performance after those choices. Repeatedly choosing improvements based on the final test makes it part of development and can make the reported result too optimistic. Match the split to the real task; time or repeated users may require special care.","professional":"Separate fitting, model-selection validation and final held-out evaluation. Iterative selection against the final test contaminates its role as an independent performance estimate. Choose time-aware or entity-aware splits when the deployment setting requires them.","hint":"Which dataset influenced your choices? A final evaluation is no longer independent if its answers repeatedly steer development.","quiz":{"question":"You try 50 variants and select the one with the best final-test score. Is that score still an independent final evaluation?","answer":"No. The test influenced model selection. Use validation for development and a suitably untouched evaluation set for the final estimate, with a split appropriate to deployment."},"source":{"name":"Google: training, validation and test sets","url":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets"},"phase":0},{"id":"registry-deployment","title":"A registry alias is not a deployment rollout","aliases":["MLflow alias deployment","registry alias rollout"],"questions":["Does changing an MLflow alias automatically update every running service?"],"career":"mlops","section":"roadmap","plain":"An MLflow model alias names a model version in the registry. Moving that alias changes which version a later lookup resolves. It does not, by itself, replace model objects already loaded by every running service. Check how your application loads or reloads models. Deploy or reload deliberately, verify the served version and keep a tested way to return to the previous version.","professional":"A registry alias is a mutable reference to a model version. Reassigning it affects subsequent resolution; it does not itself orchestrate replacement of in-memory models across serving processes. Verify loader/reload semantics, roll out explicitly, confirm served-version telemetry and retain a validated rollback path.","hint":"Trace when the service resolves the alias: at startup, on every request, or on an explicit reload?","quiz":{"question":"A service loaded a model at startup and has no reload mechanism. You move its registry alias. Must the in-memory model change immediately?","answer":"No. The alias update alone does not reload that object. Follow the service’s deployment or reload procedure and verify the version actually serving traffic."},"source":{"name":"MLflow: registry workflow","url":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/model-registry/workflow/"},"phase":2},{"id":"slo","title":"Choose a service target from user needs","aliases":["choosing an SLO","service reliability targets"],"questions":["Is 99.99% the right SLO for every AI service?"],"career":"sre","section":"roadmap","plain":"A service-level objective (SLO) is a target for a measured service behavior, such as successful requests over a stated time window. Choose it from user needs and the cost of meeting it. There is no single percentage that suits every service. State what you measure, the target and the time window. Reliability measures do not replace tests of model answer quality.","professional":"Define an SLI, target and evaluation window from user expectations and operational trade-offs. A universal 99.99% target is not justified for every workload. Use the error budget to inform reliability decisions, and evaluate model quality separately from service availability.","hint":"Who experiences the failure, how much is tolerable, and over what measurement window?","quiz":{"question":"A model API meets its availability SLO. Does that alone prove its predictions are correct?","answer":"No. Availability measures whether the service is available under its defined indicator. Prediction quality requires separate task-specific evaluation."},"source":{"name":"Google SRE: implementing SLOs","url":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/"},"phase":0}]};
